<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Scope and method</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    This report is a digest of published sources, not original research. Zunkiree Labs ran no survey and gathered no data for it. We read regulator pages and law-firm summaries for five markets, extracted what they say about two questions, and organised the results so a buyer can compare them: what AI-specific rules apply, and what happens when personal data crosses a border.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    This is general information, not legal advice. Laws and guidance change, so check each point against the current regulator page and with qualified counsel before you act. Each fact below is stated as of the date of its source. Our earlier posts report that several regulator pages were checked on 2 October 2026.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Summary: five markets side by side</h2>
  <div class="mb-5 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full min-w-[640px] text-left"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Market</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">AI-specific rules</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Cross-border data rule</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Regulator reference</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">EU (incl. Netherlands)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">EU AI Act, Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. Article 50 transparency duties apply since 2 August 2026; Annex III high-risk from 2 December 2027; Annex I from 2 August 2028.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Our sources do not set out the transfer rules; they advise asking a supplier outside the EU which transfer arrangement covers it.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens (NL); European Data Protection Board</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">United Kingdom</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">None described in our sources beyond existing data protection law.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Restricted transfers need UK adequacy regulations, appropriate safeguards with a transfer risk assessment, or an exception.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">ICO; NCSC for Cyber Essentials</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Canada</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">None described in our sources; PIPEDA applies to personal information in commercial activities.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Organisation stays responsible; contractual comparable protection; notice that data may be accessed by foreign authorities.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Australia</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Voluntary AI Safety Standard (5 September 2024), ten voluntary guardrails.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">APP 8: reasonable steps so the overseas recipient does not breach the APPs; section 16C accountability.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">OAIC; Department of Industry</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Switzerland</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">No separate AI act in our sources; the FDPIC says the Data Protection Act applies directly to AI.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Adequate-protection states listed by the Federal Council; otherwise recognised or approved standard clauses, or binding corporate rules.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">FDPIC</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    A blank or “none described” cell means our listed sources do not describe such a rule, not that none exists.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">European Union and the Netherlands</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The AI Act is Regulation (EU) 2024/1689. According to the <a href="https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/implementation-timeline/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">implementation timeline</a> (updated 31 August 2026, not the official EU text), it entered into force on 1 August 2024, with prohibitions and AI literacy requirements from 2 February 2025 and general-purpose AI model obligations from 2 August 2025.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The Digital Omnibus on AI, <a href="https://www.lewissilkin.com/insights/2026/07/27/the-digital-omnibus-on-ai-enters-into-force-today-102nedo" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">reported by Lewis Silkin</a>, was published on 24 July 2026 and entered into force on 27 July 2026. It moved Annex III high-risk obligations (for example biometrics, critical infrastructure, education, employment, credit and insurance) to 2 December 2027 and Annex I product-embedded systems to 2 August 2028. Marking of AI-generated content in systems on the market before 2 August 2026 has a grace period to 2 December 2026.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <a href="https://www.cooley.com/news/insight/2026/2026-08-03-eu-ai-act-transparency-obligations-take-effect-2-august-2026" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cooley</a> reports that Article 50 transparency duties apply from 2 August 2026, with fines of up to EUR 15 million or 3% of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher. <a href="https://www.hklaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/04/us-companies-face-eu-ai-acts-possible-august-2026-compliance-deadline" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Holland & Knight</a> describes how non-EU companies can be in scope if they place a system on the EU market or the output is used in the EU.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    In the Netherlands the Autoriteit Persoonsgegevens is the data protection authority and the Dutch member of the <a href="https://www.edpb.europa.eu/about-edpb/about-edpb/members_en" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">European Data Protection Board</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">United Kingdom</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The <a href="https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/international-transfers/international-transfers-a-guide/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ICO’s guide to international transfers</a> (updated 15 January 2026) says that a restricted transfer must be covered by UK adequacy regulations, by appropriate safeguards with a transfer risk assessment, or by an exception, and that you must not make the transfer if none applies.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    For supplier assurance, the <a href="https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/cyberessentials/overview" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCSC</a> describes Cyber Essentials as the minimum standard of cyber security recommended by the Government for organisations of all sizes. It covers five technical controls: firewalls, secure configuration, security update management, user access control and malware protection. The NCSC notes that a growing number of organisations require suppliers to be certified to bid for work.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Canada</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The Office of the Privacy Commissioner of Canada says <a href="https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/p_principle/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PIPEDA</a> sets the ground rules for how private-sector organisations collect, use and disclose personal information in commercial activities across Canada. Alberta, British Columbia and Québec have their own private-sector laws, described by the OPC as substantially similar, that apply instead in some cases for organisations operating entirely within those provinces (<a href="https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/02_05_d_15/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OPC summary of privacy laws</a>).
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    On processing abroad, the <a href="https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/airports-and-borders/gl_dab_090127/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OPC guidelines</a> (27 January 2009) say an organisation is responsible for personal information in its custody, including information transferred to a third party. It must provide a comparable level of protection, through contractual or other means (the guidelines call a contract the primary means), and must tell people the information may be sent to another jurisdiction and accessed there by courts, law enforcement and national security authorities. The OPC adds that no contract can override the criminal, national security or any other laws of the country to which the information has been transferred.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Australia</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The OAIC calls the 13 <a href="https://www.oaic.gov.au/privacy/australian-privacy-principles" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Australian Privacy Principles</a> the cornerstone of the privacy protection framework in the Privacy Act 1988. Under <a href="https://www.oaic.gov.au/privacy/australian-privacy-principles/australian-privacy-principles-guidelines/chapter-8-app-8-cross-border-disclosure-of-personal-information" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">APP 8</a>, before disclosing personal information to an overseas recipient an entity must take reasonable steps to ensure the recipient does not breach the APPs, and under section 16C it can be accountable for the recipient’s acts or practices.
  </p>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The <a href="https://www.oaic.gov.au/privacy/notifiable-data-breaches" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Notifiable Data Breaches scheme</a> requires covered organisations to notify affected individuals and the OAIC when a breach involving personal information is likely to result in serious harm.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    On AI specifically, the Australian Government’s <a href="https://www.industry.gov.au/publications/voluntary-ai-safety-standard" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Voluntary AI Safety Standard</a> was published on 5 September 2024 with ten voluntary guardrails. A later Guidance for AI Adoption, published in October 2025, updates and simplifies it, according to our earlier post; check the current version on the department’s site.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Switzerland</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The <a href="https://www.edoeb.admin.ch/en/09112023-current-data-protection-legislation-is-directly-applicable-to-ai" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDPIC</a> stated on 9 November 2023 that the Federal Act on Data Protection is formulated to apply to all types of technology and is therefore directly applicable to AI-supported data processing. It says developers, providers and users of AI applications must make the purpose, functioning and data sources of AI-based processing transparent, and that the law requires a data protection impact assessment in high-risk cases. It separately lists comprehensive real-time facial recognition and comprehensive monitoring and assessment of lifestyle (“social scoring”) as applications that are prohibited under data protection law.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    On transfers abroad, the <a href="https://www.edoeb.admin.ch/en/cross-border-transfer-of-personal-data" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDPIC</a> says disclosure is allowed where the destination state’s legislation ensures adequate protection (Art. 16(1) FADP), based on a list the Federal Council keeps in Annex 1 of the Data Protection Ordinance. Without such a decision, standard data protection clauses can be used. The FDPIC has recognised the EU standard contractual clauses and the Council of Europe model contractual clauses, while clauses drawn up by others must be approved by the FDPIC beforehand. Binding corporate rules are an option within a company group, and the FDPIC page lists further routes, so check it for your case.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">What the markets have in common</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li><strong>Accountability stays with you.</strong> Canada, Australia and the UK sources all keep the data owner responsible when a supplier or overseas team handles data.</li>
    <li><strong>Transparency.</strong> The EU Article 50 duties, the Canadian notice about foreign access and the Swiss transparency expectations all ask you to tell people what is happening.</li>
    <li><strong>Location is a risk factor.</strong> The OPC states that contracts cannot override foreign laws.</li>
    <li><strong>Dates keep moving.</strong> The EU high-risk dates were delayed in 2026, which is why each fact here carries its source date.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our own suggestion, not a legal requirement: use the table above to decide which regulator pages to read first, then put the supplier questions in our <a href="/resources/ai-compliance-business-owners-ebook/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">ebook</a> to each vendor in writing.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Limits of this digest</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
